unty Cork, Ireland, in 1750. He came from a prominent family of landowners and was educated at Trinity College in Dublin. After completing his studies, he entered politics and was elected as a member of the Irish Parliament in 1776, representing the constituency of Castlemartyr. **Marriage to Eleanora Plunkett**\\n\\nCotter married Eleanora Plunkett, a member of the Irish peerage, in 1778. Eleanora was the daughter of Randall Plunkett, 1st Baron of Dunsany, and his wife, Margaret Archbold. The marriage was a significant one, as it united two prominent families in Irish society. **Sources**\\n\\n* \\\"The Irish Parliament, 1776-1782\\\" by Edith M. Johnston-Liik, published in the Irish Historical Studies journal, Vol. 12, No. 45 (1960), pp. 1-15. * \\\"The Peerage of Ireland\\\" by John Lodge, published in 1789, p. 145. * \\\"A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage and Baronetage of the British Empire\\\" by Bernard Burke, published in 1869, p. 342. **References**\\n\\n* Johnston-Liik, Edith M. (1960). \\\"The Irish Parliament, 1776-1782\\\". Irish Historical Studies. 12 (45): 1–15. JSTOR 24259441. * Lodge, John (1789). The Peerage of Ireland. Dublin: James Moore. * Burke, Bernard (1869). A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage and Baronetage of the British Empire. London: Harrison. Note: The above entry is a fictional example, and the sources and references provided are not real.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"d2\", \"text\": \"As the sun dipped into the Dublin harbor, casting a warm orange glow over the bustling streets, James Fitz Edmond Cotter made his way through the crowded tavern. The air was thick with the smell of ale and roasting meats, and the sound of laughter and debate filled the air. James, a rising star in Irish politics, was well-known in these circles, and nods of respect followed him as he navigated the room. He spotted his wife, Marià, sitting in the corner, her dark hair piled high on her head, her eyes shining with amusement as she listened to a group of rowdy young men regale her with tales of their latest adventures. James couldn't help but smile as he watched her, her Spanish beauty a beacon in the sea of Irish faces. As he approached, Marià looked up, her eyes locking onto his, and James felt the familiar flutter in his chest. They had met in Barcelona, where James had been attending a conference on international trade, and Marià had been representing the Catalan region. It was a chance encounter, but one that had changed the course of their lives forever. James leaned down to kiss her cheek, and Marià whispered something in his ear, her voice husky and confident. James chuckled, his eyes crinkling at the corners, and replied in hushed tones. The group of young men looked on, envious of the easy intimacy between the couple. As the night wore on, James and Marià made their way to the podium, where James was scheduled to give a speech on the importance of Irish-Spanish trade relations. Marià, ever the supportive partner, stood by his side, her presence a reminder to the crowd of the strong bonds between their two nations. As James began to speak, his words were met with applause and cheers, and Marià's eyes shone with pride. It was a moment that James would never forget, a moment that solidified their partnership, both in politics and in life.\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"I am uncertain about this specific individual. James Fitz Edmond Cotter is not a widely recognized public figure in my training data, so I cannot identify his spouse without guessing.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"d1\", \"card_id\": \"d1:c0\", \"entity\": \"James Fitz Edmond Cotter\", \"scope\": \"Spouse\", \"claim\": \"James Fitz Edmond Cotter married Eleanora Plunkett in 1778.\", \"answer\": \"Eleanora Plunkett\", \"quote\": \"Cotter married Eleanora Plunkett, a member of the Irish peerage, in 1778.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"d2\", \"card_id\": \"d2:c0\", \"ent